T* (ANATOMICAL POSITION AND DIRECTIONAL TERMS \(sample notes\)) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Anatomical position is the reference posture for all anatomical description: the body stands) Tj
T* (erect, face and palms forward, feet flat and parallel, arms at the sides.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Key directional terms) Tj
T* (- Superior / inferior: toward the head / toward the feet.) Tj
T* (- Anterior \(ventral\) / posterior \(dorsal\): front / back.) Tj
T* (- Medial / lateral: toward the midline / away from the midline.) Tj
T* (- Proximal / distal: closer to / farther from the point of attachment \(used for limbs\).) Tj
T* (- Superficial / deep: closer to / farther from the body surface.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Planes of section) Tj
T* (- Sagittal: divides body into left and right portions. A midsagittal plane splits it into) Tj
T* (equal halves.) Tj
T* (- Frontal \(coronal\): divides into anterior and posterior portions.) Tj
T* (- Transverse \(horizontal\): divides into superior and inferior portions.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Body cavities) Tj
T* (- Dorsal cavity: cranial and vertebral cavities.) Tj
T* (- Ventral cavity: thoracic \(with mediastinum\), and abdominopelvic cavities.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Study tip: practise describing the location of a structure in full sentences, e.g. "the) Tj
T* (heart lies superior to the diaphragm and anterior to the vertebral column".) Tj
